Chapter 1. Introduction

1.1 The problem

Educational AI systems are increasingly deployed at scale. Payment for such systems, when it is tied to outcomes at all, is typically tied to verified scores, completion rates, or engagement metrics. These metrics can be manipulated by the provider, and in the absence of a formal treatment of strategic behavior, the settlement rule cannot be trusted to align with true learning.

The problem has three layers. The first is measurement: what quantity should be measured. The second is identification: whether the causal effect of the intervention on that quantity can be inferred from available data. The third is incentive compatibility: whether a provider who knows the settlement rule can improve their payment without improving the underlying educational outcome.

Existing work in outcome-based financing (OBF) addresses the first two layers. Existing work in causal inference provides tools for the second layer. Existing work in mechanism design provides the language of the third layer. But no formal framework composes all three into a single settlement protocol with an explicit collapse condition.

The central question of this dissertation is:

Under what conditions can a causal effect on learning be transformed into an economically settleable event, robustly with respect to a strategic provider who knows the protocol?

1.2 Epistemic discipline

Three commitments govern the entire analysis.

(D1) Collapse allowed. The protocol may return $\varnothing$, meaning "no verified value." This is not a failure but a central design feature. If identification fails, if the counterfactual is indefensible, if gaming risk is unbounded, or if verification cost exceeds captured value, the correct response is $\varnothing$, not a spurious estimate.

(D2) No undemonstrated bounds. No inequality is stated in the main results unless it is proven under explicitly stated hypotheses. Approximations are labeled as such.

(D3) Separation of concerns. Causal validity, incentive compatibility, verification cost, and equity are distinct feasibility regions. The CLVP is viable only in their intersection.

