Chapter 3. Formal Framework

3.1 Primitives

Let $\mathcal{N}$ denote a finite set of learners, each with an unobserved sensitivity $s_i > 0$. Let $X_t$ denote the learner's state at time $t$, $A_t$ the intervention, and $Y_t$ the measured outcome. Counterfactual outcomes are written $Y_i(\pi)$ for a policy $\pi$. The causal gain for a population $P$ is

\tau_{\mathcal{I}} = \mathbb{E}_{i \sim P}[Y_i(\pi) - Y_i(\pi_0)].

3.2 Evaluation unit

The evaluation unit is the tuple

\mathcal{I} = (P, \pi, \pi_0, L, T, C, D, V)

with $P$ population, $\pi$ evaluated policy, $\pi_0$ baseline, $L$ learning functional, $T$ horizon, $C$ context, $D$ identification design, $V$ verification protocol.

3.3 Settlement function

Let $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{valid}}$ denote the set of evaluation units satisfying conditions (C1)–(C10) below. The settlement function is

\Phi : \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{valid}} \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \cup \{\varnothing\}.

We set $\Phi(\mathcal{I}) = \varnothing$ if any of the following fail:

· (C1) Identification. The causal gain $\tau_{\mathcal{I}}$ is identifiable under $D$ and its assumptions $A(D)$.
· (C2) Defensible counterfactual. The counterfactual $Y(\pi_0)$ is defensible under $D$.
· (C3) Determinate attribution. The effect attributable to the evaluated intervention is determinate.
· (C4) Specification stability. The effect is stable across reasonable specifications.
· (C5) Bounded gaming risk. The risk of metric gaming is bounded.
· (C6) Evaluator independence. Provider $\neq$ Evaluator.
· (C7) Transfer and retention validity. The effect persists under transfer and delayed testing.
· (C8) Pre-specified horizon. The evaluation horizon is fixed ex ante.
· (C9) Equity constraint. Any group-level constraint $\tau_g \geq \tau_{\min}$ is satisfied.
· (C10) Verification cost. Verification cost is strictly less than captured value.

3.4 Three layers

The model has three layers.

Learning technology. $\Delta L_i = e_i s_i + \varepsilon_i$, where $e_i$ is provider effort and $\varepsilon_i$ is idiosyncratic noise.

Measurement technology. $Y_i = \Delta L_i + h_g \cdot g + \eta_i$, where $h_g > 0$ is the gaming effectiveness on the payment score, $g$ is the gaming intensity, and $\eta_i$ is measurement noise.

Verification technology. The probability of detecting gaming is $p(g) = 1 - e^{-\kappa g}$, with $\kappa$ derived from verification resources in Chapter 10.

The parameters $h_g$ (gaming effectiveness on the payment score) and $q$ (gaming detectability in the verification instrument) are distinct. They coincide only when the same instrument serves both roles. The metric security ratio $\rho_M = q/h_g$ captures the relationship.

