Chapter 11. Viability Frontier

11.1 Social value

Let $v > \lambda$. At IC equilibrium,

V(\kappa) = v \cdot \Delta L^* - S^* - C_{\mathrm{ver}}(\kappa) - C_{\mathrm{admin}},

with $\Delta L^* = \lambda s^2/c_e$ and $S^* = \lambda^2 s^2/c_e$. Hence

V(\kappa) = \Pi - c_{\mathrm{test}} \kappa^2, \qquad \Pi := \frac{(v - \lambda) \lambda s^2}{c_e} - c_0 - C_{\mathrm{admin}}.

11.2 Viability region

Definition 11.2.1. The CLVP viability region is

\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{CLVP}} := \{\kappa > 0 : \kappa \geq \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} \text{ and } V(\kappa) \geq 0\}.

Theorem C (Verification Viability Frontier). Assume $\Pi > 0$. Then $\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{CLVP}} \neq \varnothing$ if and only if $\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}^2 \leq \Pi / c_{\mathrm{test}}$. Equivalently, defining

\Xi := \frac{\Pi}{c_{\mathrm{test}} \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}^2},

the viability region is non-empty if and only if $\Xi \geq 1$. When $\Xi > 1$, the region is the non-degenerate interval $[\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}, \kappa_{\max}]$ with $\kappa_{\max} = \sqrt{\Pi / c_{\mathrm{test}}}$.

Proof. The intersection of $[\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}, \infty)$ with $[0, \kappa_{\max}]$ is non-empty if and only if $\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} \leq \kappa_{\max}$. Squaring gives $\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}^2 \leq \Pi / c_{\mathrm{test}}$, equivalently $\Xi \geq 1$. Strict positivity of the interval requires strict inequality. $\square$

11.3 Explicit form of $\Xi$

Substituting $\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} = h_g c_e/(\lambda s^2)$ and $c_{\mathrm{test}} = c_1 \sigma_\eta^2 z_\alpha^2 / q^2$:

\Xi = \frac{\Pi \lambda^2 s^4 q^2}{c_1 \sigma_\eta^2 z_\alpha^2 h_g^2 c_e^2}.

In the benchmark $\Pi = \lambda^2 s^2/c_e$:

\Xi_{\mathrm{benchmark}} = \frac{\lambda^4 s^6 q^2}{c_1 \sigma_\eta^2 z_\alpha^2 h_g^2 c_e^3} = \Lambda \cdot \rho_M^2,

where

\Lambda = \frac{\lambda^4 s^6}{c_1 \sigma_\eta^2 z_\alpha^2 c_e^3}, \qquad \rho_M = \frac{q}{h_g}.

11.4 Interpretation

The viability number factors into a base viability index $\Lambda$ depending only on pedagogical and economic primitives, and the square of the metric security ratio $\rho_M$ depending on the verification architecture.

Corollary 11.4.1. Causal validity does not imply viability. Taking $h_g \to \infty$ with $q$ fixed gives $\rho_M \to 0$, hence $\Xi \to 0$, even though identification is available.

Corollary 11.4.2. $\partial \Xi_{\mathrm{benchmark}} / \partial q > 0$ and $\partial \Xi_{\mathrm{benchmark}} / \partial h_g < 0$.

