Chapter 12. Endogeneity

12.1 Two distinct endogeneities

A strategic provider may influence (i) the gaming effectiveness $h_g$, by choosing the measurement instrument, and (ii) the gaming detectability $q$, by choosing how verification is conducted. These are separate.

12.2 Endogenous $h_g$

Theorem D1 (Metric Endogeneity Destroys Viability). Suppose the provider chooses $h_g \in [h_g^{\min}, \infty)$ while $q$ remains fixed by the evaluator. Then for any $\Xi_0 > 0$, there exists $h_g^*$ such that $h_g > h_g^*$ implies $\Xi < 1$.

Proof. In the benchmark, $\Xi = \Lambda q^2 / h_g^2$ is strictly decreasing in $h_g$. As $h_g \to \infty$, $\Xi \to 0$. Taking $h_g^* = q \sqrt{\Lambda}$ gives the result. $\square$

Corollary 12.2.1. A necessary condition for viability is that the provider cannot select the instrument. Institutionally, this is the condition $Provider \neq Metric\ Designer$.

12.3 Endogenous $q$

Theorem D2 (Detectability Endogeneity Also Destroys Viability). Suppose the provider chooses $q \in (0, q^{\max}]$ while $h_g$ remains fixed. Then $q \to 0$ implies $\Xi \to 0$.

Proof. $\Xi = \Lambda q^2 / h_g^2$ is monotone increasing in $q$. $\square$

12.4 Joint endogeneity

Corollary 12.4.1. Joint endogeneity of $h_g$ and $q$ implies $\Xi = 0$ in the limit as $h_g \to \infty$ and $q \to 0$.

12.5 Metric security ratio as a design primitive

$\rho_M$ Regime
$\geq 1$ Verification amplifies gaming signal
$\approx 1$ Symmetric
$\ll 1$ Verification dominated by gaming

The design goal is to maximize $\rho_M$ subject to verification cost.

