Chapter 14. Adaptive Policies and Off-Policy Evaluation

14.1 Setup

Given logs $\{(x_t, a_t, r_t)\}$ generated under a behavior policy $\pi_b$, the target is to estimate

V(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{x \sim d^\pi}[r(x, \pi(x))].

The standard importance-weighted estimator is

\hat{V}_{\mathrm{IPS}}(\pi) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_t \frac{\pi(a_t | x_t)}{\pi_b(a_t | x_t)} r_t.

14.2 Admissibility

Theorem F (OPE Admissibility as Identification). Suppose there exists $(x, a)$ with $\pi(a | x) > 0$ and $\pi_b(a | x) = 0$. Then:

1. The IPS estimator is undefined on trajectories containing $(x, a)$.
2. Even if a regularized estimator is used, $V(\pi)$ is not identified from data generated under $\pi_b$.

Proof.

(1) The IPS weight is $1/0$.

(2) Any estimator based on $\pi_b$-data relies on the change-of-measure identity

V(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{x \sim d^{\pi_b}} \left[ \frac{\pi(a | x)}{\pi_b(a | x)} r(x, a) \right].

This identity requires absolute continuity $\pi \ll \pi_b$. If $\pi_b(a | x) = 0$ and $\pi(a | x) > 0$, absolute continuity fails, the right-hand side is undefined, and no consistent estimator exists. $\square$

Corollary 14.2.1. A necessary condition for OPE-based CLVP settlement is

\mathrm{Supp}(\pi) \subseteq \mathrm{Supp}(\pi_b).

Corollary 14.2.2. If the provider controls $\pi_b$, the provider can render any target policy $\pi$ non-identifiable by selecting $\pi_b$ with support disjoint from $\pi$. The correct response is $\Phi(\mathcal{I}) = \varnothing$.

14.3 Open problem for adaptive policies

Characterizing the IC threshold $\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}$ for adaptive policies, where the state distribution itself depends on the policy, is an open problem. Conjecturally, the threshold depends on the complexity of the policy class and the effective horizon.

