C.1 Proof of Theorem H (Linear Vanishing)

Statement. As $\kappa \uparrow \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}$,

\lim_{\kappa \uparrow \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}} \frac{g^*(\kappa)}{\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} - \kappa} = \frac{\lambda^2 s^2}{c_e c_g}.

Proof.

Consider the function $G(g, \kappa) = U_g(g, \kappa)$. The non-IC equilibrium satisfies $G(g^*(\kappa), \kappa) = 0$ for $\kappa < \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}$.

At the boundary point $(0, \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}})$:

G(0, \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}) = U'(0; \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}) = b - 2a\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} = b - 2a \cdot \frac{b}{2a} = 0.

Compute the partial derivatives of $G$ at $(0, \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}})$.

Partial in $g$:

G_g = U''(0; \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}).

From the earlier computation (Appendix A.1, Case 3), $U''(0; \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}) = -2c = -c_g$.

Partial in $\kappa$:

G_\kappa = \frac{\partial}{\partial \kappa} U'(g, \kappa)\bigg|_{g = 0, \kappa = \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}}.

Recall

U'(g, \kappa) = -2a\kappa e^{-2\kappa g} + b e^{-\kappa g}(1 - \kappa g) - 2cg.

At $g = 0$:

U'(0, \kappa) = -2a\kappa + b.

Therefore:

\frac{\partial U'}{\partial \kappa}(0, \kappa) = -2a.

At $\kappa = \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}$:

G_\kappa = -2a.

Implicit function theorem. Since $G_g = -c_g \neq 0$, by the implicit function theorem:

\frac{dg^*}{d\kappa} = -\frac{G_\kappa}{G_g} = -\frac{-2a}{-c_g} = -\frac{2a}{c_g}.

Substituting $2a = \lambda^2 s^2/c_e$:

\frac{dg^*}{d\kappa} = -\frac{\lambda^2 s^2}{c_e c_g}.

Integrating from $\kappa$ to $\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}$:

g^*(\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}) - g^*(\kappa) = -\frac{\lambda^2 s^2}{c_e c_g}(\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} - \kappa) + o(\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} - \kappa).

Since $g^*(\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}) = 0$:

g^*(\kappa) = \frac{\lambda^2 s^2}{c_e c_g}(\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} - \kappa) + o(\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} - \kappa). \quad \square

