Appendix I. Proofs for Chapter 13: Institutional Locks

I.1 Proof of Theorem E (Lock Necessity, Weakened Form)

Statement. For each lock $L_j$, there exists a parameter configuration $\omega_j$ such that the CLVP admits a manipulation strategy with positive expected gain for the provider and non-positive effect on true learning.

Proof.

Case $L_1$ (Population lock removed). Consider heterogeneous sensitivities $s_i \in \{s_L, s_H\}$ with $s_L < s_H$. Suppose the provider can choose $P_A$ after observing $s_i$. Selecting $P_A = \{i : s_i = s_H\}$ maximizes expected score per unit effort. Let $|P_A| = N_H$. The provider's expected utility is

U_A = e^{-\kappa g}\lambda(e \bar{s}_H + h_g g) N_H - \frac{c_e}{2}e^2 - \frac{c_g}{2}g^2.

With all $s_H$ students selected, the score per student increases. But the total learning in the full population decreases because low-$s$ students receive no effort. Formally, if the social objective is $\sum_i \Delta L_i$ over the full population, then

\Delta L_{\mathrm{social}} = e s_H N_H < e s_H N_H + e s_L N_L = \Delta L_{\mathrm{full}}

(the right side is the counterfactual with all students treated). So $\Delta L_{\mathrm{social}} - \Delta L_{\mathrm{full}} < 0$, but the provider's payment may still be positive because the payment depends only on $P_A$.

Case $L_2$ (Design lock removed). Let the provider choose the comparison group. Suppose there are two candidate comparison groups: a "strong" one with mean outcome $\mu_S$ and a "weak" one with mean outcome $\mu_W < \mu_S$. The treatment effect estimate is $\hat{\tau} = \bar{Y}_{\mathrm{treat}} - \bar{Y}_{\mathrm{control}}$. Choosing the weak comparison group inflates $\hat{\tau}$ by $\mu_S - \mu_W > 0$ without changing the true causal effect.

Case $L_3$ (Metric lock removed). Apply Theorem D1. The provider selects $h_g > q\sqrt{\Lambda}$, giving $\Xi < 1$.

Case $L_4$ (Held-out verification removed). The provider optimizes on $D_{\mathrm{train}}$. On a held-out set $D_{\mathrm{holdout}}$, the estimate $\hat{V}(\pi)$ may exceed $\hat{V}(\pi_0)$ without generalization. This requires only that the overfitting gap be positive on the specific holdout.

Case $L_5$ (Randomized audit removed). Suppose audits are deterministic at time $T$. The provider games at $t < T - \epsilon$ and stops gaming before $T$. Detection is measured only at $T$, giving $p \approx 0$. The true learning is not improved by the pre-audit gaming. $\square$

