Appendix L. Detailed Derivations

L.1 Derivation of $e^{*\prime}(0)$

Starting from $C_e'(e^*(g)) = \lambda s e^{-\kappa g}$, differentiate both sides with respect to $g$:

C_e''(e^*(g)) \cdot e^{*\prime}(g) = -\kappa \lambda s e^{-\kappa g}.

At $g = 0$:

e^{*\prime}(0) = -\frac{\kappa \lambda s}{C_e''(e_0)}.

For quadratic costs with $C_e''(e_0) = c_e$:

e^{*\prime}(0) = -\frac{\kappa \lambda s}{c_e}. \quad \square

L.2 Derivation of the Second Derivative $U''(0)$ (General Case)

Starting from

U'(g) = \lambda e^{-\kappa g}[h_g - \kappa(s e^*(g) + h_g g)] - C_g'(g),

differentiate with respect to $g$:

U''(g) = -\kappa \lambda e^{-\kappa g}[h_g - \kappa(s e^*(g) + h_g g)] + \lambda e^{-\kappa g}[-\kappa(s e^{*\prime}(g) + h_g)] - C_g''(g).

At $g = 0$:

U''(0) = -\kappa \lambda h_g + \lambda[-\kappa(s e^{*\prime}(0) + h_g)] - C_g''(0)


= -\kappa \lambda h_g - \kappa \lambda s e^{*\prime}(0) - \kappa \lambda h_g - C_g''(0)


= -2\kappa \lambda h_g - \kappa \lambda s \cdot \left(-\frac{\kappa \lambda s}{C_e''(e_0)}\right) - C_g''(0)


= -2\kappa \lambda h_g + \frac{\kappa^2 \lambda^2 s^2}{C_e''(e_0)} - C_g''(0).

This can be written as

U''(0) = \lambda \kappa^2 s e_0 - 2\kappa \lambda h_g + \frac{\kappa^2 \lambda^2 s^2}{C_e''(e_0)} - C_g''(0) - \lambda \kappa^2 s e_0.

Using the boundary relation $\kappa_{\mathrm{FOC}} = h_g/(s e_0)$, we have $\kappa s e_0 = h_g$ at the boundary, so

\lambda \kappa^2 s e_0 = \lambda \kappa h_g.

Therefore at the boundary:

U''(0) = \lambda \kappa h_g - 2\kappa \lambda h_g + \frac{\kappa^2 \lambda^2 s^2}{C_e''(e_0)} - C_g''(0) = -\kappa \lambda h_g + \frac{\kappa^2 \lambda^2 s^2}{C_e''(e_0)} - C_g''(0).

Substituting $\kappa = h_g/(s e_0)$:

-\kappa \lambda h_g = -\frac{\lambda h_g^2}{s e_0}, \qquad \frac{\kappa^2 \lambda^2 s^2}{C_e''(e_0)} = \frac{\lambda^2 h_g^2}{e_0^2 C_e''(e_0)}.

So

U''(0) = -\frac{\lambda h_g^2}{s e_0} + \frac{\lambda^2 h_g^2}{e_0^2 C_e''(e_0)} - C_g''(0). \quad \square

L.3 Derivation of the Composed Slope for Theorem H

From $U_{g\kappa}(0, \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}) = -2a$ and $U_{gg}(0, \kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}) = -c_g$:

\frac{dg^*}{d\kappa} = -\frac{-2a}{-c_g} = -\frac{2a}{c_g}.

With $2a = \lambda^2 s^2/c_e$:

\frac{dg^*}{d\kappa} = -\frac{\lambda^2 s^2}{c_e c_g}.

The sign is negative, meaning that as $\kappa$ increases toward $\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}$, $g^*$ decreases toward zero. The slope is proportional to $\lambda^2 s^2$ and inversely proportional to $c_e c_g$. $\square$

L.4 Derivation of the Viability Factor Decomposition

Start from $\Xi = \Pi/(c_{\mathrm{test}}\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}^2)$ with $\Pi = \lambda^2 s^2/c_e$, $c_{\mathrm{test}} = c_1 \sigma_\eta^2 z_\alpha^2/q^2$, $\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} = h_g c_e/(\lambda s^2)$.

Numerator: $\Pi = \lambda^2 s^2/c_e$.

Denominator: $c_{\mathrm{test}}\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}}^2 = \frac{c_1 \sigma_\eta^2 z_\alpha^2}{q^2} \cdot \frac{h_g^2 c_e^2}{\lambda^2 s^4}$.

Ratio:

\Xi = \frac{\lambda^2 s^2/c_e}{c_1 \sigma_\eta^2 z_\alpha^2 h_g^2 c_e^2/(q^2 \lambda^2 s^4)} = \frac{\lambda^2 s^2}{c_e} \cdot \frac{q^2 \lambda^2 s^4}{c_1 \sigma_\eta^2 z_\alpha^2 h_g^2 c_e^2} = \frac{\lambda^4 s^6 q^2}{c_1 \sigma_\eta^2 z_\alpha^2 h_g^2 c_e^3}.

Define

\Lambda = \frac{\lambda^4 s^6}{c_1 \sigma_\eta^2 z_\alpha^2 c_e^3}, \qquad \rho_M = \frac{q}{h_g}.

Then

\Xi = \Lambda \cdot \rho_M^2. \quad \square

L.5 Derivation of the Welfare Loss Coefficient

From Theorem J′: $\mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{L}} = (v - \lambda)\lambda^2 s^2 h_g/(c_e c_g)$.

Substituting $\kappa_{\mathrm{crit}} = h_g c_e/(\lambda s^2)$:

\mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{L}} = (v - \lambda) \cdot \frac{\lambda^2 s^2}{c_e c_g} \cdot h_g = (v - \lambda) \lambda^2 s^2 \cdot \frac{h_g}{c_e c_g}.

The coefficient is linear in $v - \lambda$ and $h_g$, and inversely proportional to $c_e c_g$. $\square$

L.6 Derivation of the Deviation Function Monotonicity

From the proof of Theorem B:

\frac{\partial D}{\partial \kappa}(g, \kappa) = -2a g e^{-2\kappa g} - b g^2 e^{-\kappa g}.

Every factor is positive except the leading minus signs. Hence the derivative is strictly negative for all $g > 0$ and $\kappa > 0$. $\square$

L.7 Derivation of the Exponential Approximation for Detection

For a one-sided test with statistic $z = qg\sqrt{mn}/\sigma_\eta$ and critical value $z_\alpha$:

p(g) = \Phi(z - z_\alpha).

For large $z_\alpha$ and small $z - z_\alpha$, the normal CDF tail is approximately

\Phi(z - z_\alpha) \approx 1 - e^{-(z_\alpha + 0.5/z_\alpha)(z - z_\alpha)}

Using the Mills ratio approximation. Setting $\kappa = (z_\alpha + 0.5/z_\alpha) q\sqrt{mn}/\sigma_\eta$ gives $p(g) \approx 1 - e^{-\kappa g}$. $\square$

